window.__COPY = {"drivers.hint.more":"","drivers.lead":"A driver is just another word for a reason. Different risks often share the same causes and that matters for what the House decides.","enriched.s1.p0":"Members said each risk in their cluster in their own words","enriched.s2.p0":"Members were asked: What about this risk stands out to you? They could think of a risk as a hazard, the people or things exposed to it, and what makes them vulnerable.","foot":"","futures.lead2":"Each common future rises over the risk map, with connections to the risks it seeks to answer.","impacts.hm.foot":"The warmer the colour, the higher the combined score, indicating the risk affects the area strongly.","impacts.hm.lead":"The results of everyone's voting formed the all-groups heatmap.","impacts.hm.note":"Each box is the average across the groups that scored that risk. Read down a column to see how much lands on one part of public life.","impacts.hm.swipe":"","impacts.lead":"Members met the 13 risks from the expert horizon scan and asked, *if a risk got out of hand, what part of society would it land on?*","impacts.why.l":"Welcome","priorities.blead":"Members saw two drivers at a time and chose the one the House should spend its time on first. Then each member picked up to five of the 27 risks to prioritise.","priorities.lead":"Each room read three or four risks from one grouping of the risk map and talked about *what we want to achieve and why*. Then members ranked the drivers and picked the risks they most want the House to spend time on.","priorities.rnote":"","riskmap.fold.t":"The Odyssean Institute's presentation: how the horizon scan created this risk map","riskmap.s9.p0":"The risk map is a framework to understand the evidence gathered by the Odyssean Institute during the horizon scan. The map helps you explore what these risks mean and what kinds of people are affected by them.","s1.lead":"Session 1 was the welcome session, where members met each other, started from their own experience of AI and asked: what actually is AI?","s1.p0.l":"_","s1.p1.l":"What a citizens' assembly is, where the House of the People has come from and why this second sitting is about AI.","s1.p2.l":"Scientists, researchers, campaigners and even previous members of the House of the People are excited to support the recommendations from the House. Four of them spoke to members.","s1.p5.l":"A poll asking 'How often do you use AI' and whether members agreed that: 1) AI poses dangers to society, 2) AI will help society advance in many ways and 3) AI will become very important, generations from now.","s1.p5.t":"Four Quick Questions","s1.p6.l":"Analogies about how AI works on a fundamental level to better understand what's going on under the bonnet. The presentation was put together with help from the Odyssean Institute, who made sure that it's all technically right.","s1.para":"Members heard *where the House of the People has come from*; and four guest voices explaining *why this sitting is important*. In small groups, members met each other and talked about *what AI is to us*, where we encounter it and how we feel about where it's going. Then, a look under the bonnet at *what actually is AI*. Finally, members finished by describing AI in their own words and sharing the questions they most want answered.","s1.t1.h":"","s1.t2.h":"","s2.p1.l":"What AI is able to do and how AI is being used: how capable it is, what AI agents are, what happens if you give thousands of super-powerful AI agents an impossible puzzle, where AI is in our daily lives, and how AI is used in companies like Palantir.","s2.p2.l":"Dr Frank Sauer spoke first, on AI in the military; his talk is described here in the words of the military handout members were given.","s2.p7.l":"One question per topic from all the rooms was put it to the guest speakers. Members asked how schools can tell whether a student has genuinely learned and produced their own work, rather than using AI; what meaningful human control must remain over AI-enabled weapons and lethal decisions, especially as systems act faster or more autonomously; what retraining, education and funding arrangements will support workers who are displaced or affected by AI, including whether technology companies should contribute; and how patients will know when AI has informed a diagnosis or treatment, and who remains accountable for checking or overruling it.","s2.para":"A presentation on what AI is able to do and how it is being used. Four guest speakers then gave short talks on AI in the military, the workplace, healthcare and education. In small groups, members talked about what in their topic was most important to them and what they felt was missing, and some questions went to the guest speakers afterwards.","s3.p0.l":"","s3.p1.l":"The vast, planetary-sized network behind every question you ask a chatbot: the materials from the earth, the labour of people and the information in our data, and the data centres, water and electricity it all runs on.","s3.p2.l":"An example card on AI image creation tool: in the past you could find an image in a free library, buy a photo from a stock library, or pay an artist or creator to make it, and today a lot of people get an image quickly using AI instead, trained on the works of the very artists that the AI is now replacing. Each card looks at what was happening before AI, what is happening now with AI, and what would still be happening without AI, and asks: is the output worth the input?","s3.p4.l":"Back in the main room, members rated how important each input was in a poll, and heard summaries of what the groups had talked about on each card.","s3.para":"Session 2 asked how AI is used; this session asked what it takes to use it — and what would be happening without it. A presentation showed what happens in the one second between asking a chatbot a question and getting an answer. In small groups, members each read and considered one of four encounter cards: a home assistant, an AI-generated advert, AI document review and an AI customer support line.","s4.a1.h":"The future, and members' hopes and fears","s4.g0.n":"","s4.poll.f":"","s4.poll.hover":"","s4.poll.l":"Members answered four quick questions in a Zoom poll:","tl.intro":"Here is all of the contents of the education phase of the AI sitting: the presentations, guest speakers, handouts, small group activities and members' own notes and words.","tl.s9.name":"governance & power"};
